{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --find 'normalization' --lines 60\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 271,\n  \"next_line\": 331,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\\n290: For example, with addition of fine-grained features, such as features of goods_ids with dimensionality of 0.6 billion (including v​i​s​i​t​e​d​_​g​o​o​d​s​_​i​d​svisited\\\\_goods\\\\_ids of user and g​o​o​d​s​_​i​dgoods\\\\_id of ad as described in Table 1), model performance falls rapidly after the first epoch during training without regularization, as the dark green line shown in Fig.4 in later section 6.5.\\n291: It is not practical to directly apply traditional regularization methods, such as ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} and ℓ1\\\\ell_{1} regularization, on training networks with sparse inputs and hundreds of millions of parameters.\\n292: Take ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization as an example.\\n293: Only parameters of non-zero sparse features appearing in each mini-batch needs to be updated in the scenario of SGD based optimization methods without regularization.\\n294: However, when adding ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization it needs to calculate L2-norm over the whole parameters for each mini-batch, which leads to extremely heavy computations and is unacceptable with parameters scaling up to hundreds of millions.\\n295: Figure 3. Control function of PReLU and Dice.\\n296: In this paper, we introduce an efficient mini-batch aware regularizer, which only calculates the L2-norm over the parameters of sparse features appearing in each mini-batch and makes the computation possible.\\n297: In fact, it is the embedding dictionary that contributes most of the parameters for CTR networks and arises the difficulty of heavy computation.\\n298: Let 𝐖∈ℝD×K\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D\\\\times K} denote parameters of the whole embedding dictionary, with DD as the dimensionality of the embedding vector and KK as the dimensionality of feature space.\\n299: Expand the ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization on 𝐖\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}} over samples\\n300: (4)\\n301: L2​(𝐖)=‖𝐖‖22=∑j=1K‖𝒘j‖22=∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮∑j=1KI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})&=\\\\|\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}=\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\\\end{split}\\n302: where 𝒘j∈ℝD\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D} is the jj-th embedding vector,\\n303: I⁡(𝒙j≠0)I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0) denotes if the instance 𝒙\\\\bm{x} has the feature id jj, and njn_{j} denotes the number of occurrence for feature id jj in all samples. Eq.(4) can be transformed into Eq.(5) in the mini-batch aware manner\\n304: (5)\\n305: L2​(𝐖)=∑j=1K∑m=1B∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬmI⁡(𝒙j≠0)nj​‖𝒘j‖22,L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}\\\\frac{I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2},\\n306: where BB denotes the number of mini-batches, ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m} denotes the mm-th mini-batch.\\n307: Let αm​j=max(𝒙,y)∈ℬm⁡I⁡(𝒙j≠0)\\\\alpha_{mj}=\\\\max_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)\\n308: denote if there is at least one instance having the feature id jj in mini-batch ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}.\\n309: Then Eq.(5) can be approximated by\\n310: (6)\\n311: L2​(𝐖)≈∑j=1K∑m=1Bαm​jnj​‖𝒘j‖22.L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})\\\\approx\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}.\\n312: In this way, we derive an approximated mini-batch aware version of ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization.\\n313: For the mm-th mini-batch, the gradient w.r.t. the embedding weights of feature jj is\\n314: (7)\\n315: 𝒘j←𝒘j−η⁡[1|ℬm|​∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬm∂L⁡(p⁡(𝒙),y)∂𝒘j+λ​αm​jnj​𝒘j],\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\leftarrow\\\\bm{w}_{j}-\\\\eta\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{|\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}|}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}{\\\\frac{\\\\partial L(p(\\\\bm{x}),y)}{\\\\partial\\\\bm{w}_{j}}+\\\\lambda\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\bm{w}_{j}}\\\\right],\\n316: in which only parameters of features appearing in mm-th mini-batch participate in the computation of regularization.\\n317: 5.2. Data Adaptive Activation Function\\n318: PReLU (He\\n319: et al., 2015) is a commonly used activation function\\n320: (8)\\n321: f⁡(s)={s if ​s>0α​s if ​s≤0.=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,f(s)=\\\\begin{cases}s&\\\\text{ if }s>0\\\\\\\\\\n322: \\\\alpha s&\\\\text{ if }s\\\\leq 0.\\\\end{cases}=\\\\penalty\\\\ p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\n323: where ss is one dimension of the input of activation function f⁡(⋅)f(\\\\cdot) and p⁡(s)=I⁡(s>0)p(s)=I(s>0) is an indicator function which controls f⁡(s)f(s) to switch between two channels of f⁡(s)=sf(s)=s and f⁡(s)=α​sf(s)=\\\\alpha s. α\\\\alpha in the second channel is a learning parameter.\\n324: Here we refer to p⁡(s)p(s) as the control function. The left part of Fig.3 plots the control function of PReLU.\\n325: PReLU takes a hard rectified point with value of 00, which may be not suitable when the inputs of each layer follow different distributions.\\n326: Take this into consideration, we design a novel data adaptive activation function named Dice,\\n327: (9)\\n328: f⁡(s)=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,p⁡(s)=11+e−s−E⁡[s]V​a​r​[s]+ϵf(s)=p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\\\ \\\\ p(s)=\\\\frac{1}{1+e^{-\\\\frac{s-E[s]}{\\\\sqrt{Var[s]+\\\\epsilon}}}}\\n329: with the control function to be plotted in the right part of Fig.3.\\n330: In the training phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is the mean and variance of input in each mini-batch.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
